---
import { getCollection } from "astro:content";
import { ClientRouter } from "astro:transitions";
import CommandPalette from "../components/CommandPalette.astro";
import "../styles/global.css";

// Validated at build time by src/content.config.ts.
const all = await getCollection("candidates");
const candidates = all.map((e) => e.data);
const websites = candidates.flatMap((c) => c.websites);
const audited = websites.filter((w) => w.audit);
const live = audited.filter((w) => w.audit?.statusClass === "live");
const dead = audited.filter((w) => ["dns_error", "connection_error"].includes(w.audit?.statusClass ?? ""));
const onchain = candidates.filter((c) => c.onchain);
---

<html lang="en-GB">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
    <meta
      name="description"
      content="How Civicord is built: Campaign Lab's 2025 scrape of 2,375 UK candidate websites, an independent liveness audit, and permanent ENS-anchored public records."
    />
    <meta property="og:title" content="Methodology — Civicord" />
    <meta
      property="og:description"
      content="Where Civicord's data comes from, how the liveness audit works, and what the on-chain public record means."
    />
    <meta property="og:type" content="article" />
    <meta name="twitter:card" content="summary" />
    <link rel="icon" type="image/svg+xml" href="/favicon.svg" />
    <title>Methodology — Civicord</title>
    <ClientRouter />
  </head>
  <body class="reveal-root method">
    <p class="crumb"><a href="/">← Public record index</a></p>

    <header class="masthead">
      <h1 class="wordmark">Method<span class="dot">.</span></h1>
      <p class="lede">What Civicord claims, where it comes from, and how you can check it yourself.</p>
    </header>

    <article>
      <h2>1. The source: Campaign Lab scrape, April 2025</h2>
      <p>
        Every record starts from Campaign Lab's scrape of UK parliamentary
        candidate campaign websites, captured during the 2024 general election
        campaign. The dataset covers <b>{candidates.length.toLocaleString("en-GB")} candidates</b> and{" "}
        <b>{websites.length.toLocaleString("en-GB")} websites</b>, keyed on Democracy Club
        person IDs — the stable public identifier for UK candidates.
      </p>

      <h2>2. The audit: re-checked after the election</h2>
      <p>
        Websites rot. Roughly 17 months after the scrape we re-requested every
        URL and recorded what happened: <b>{live.length.toLocaleString("en-GB")} still live</b>,{" "}
        <b>{dead.length.toLocaleString("en-GB")} domains gone or unreachable</b>, the rest
        redirecting, erroring, or timing out.
      </p>
      <p>“Live” is deliberately strict — it means the site responds <em>and</em> still mentions the candidate by name. A live domain selling insurance or redirecting to a party homepage does not count.</p>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Status</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Live</td><td>Responds and still mentions the candidate</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Domain gone</td><td>DNS fails or connection refused</td></tr>
          <tr><td>HTTP error</td><td>Server responds with 4xx/5xx</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Timed out / SSL / Conn. failed</td><td>Unreachable for technical reasons — retry before concluding</td></tr>
          <tr><td>↳ redirect</td><td>URL now sends visitors elsewhere (destination shown)</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h2>3. The permanent record: ENS names</h2>
      <p>
        <b>{onchain.length.toLocaleString("en-GB")} of {candidates.length.toLocaleString("en-GB")} candidates</b> have
        a permanent public name of the form <code>p1234.civicord.eth</code> on the
        Ethereum Name Service (Sepolia testnet). Each name carries three public text
        records — the campaign website URL, the audit status, and the candidate's
        name — readable by anyone, without an account, a wallet, or our permission.
      </p>
      <p>
        Once written, records can't be quietly edited or deleted: any change is a
        new on-chain transaction with a public history. That is the whole point —
        a candidate (or anyone else) cannot rewrite what their site once said.
      </p>
      <p>
        To verify a record yourself: open any candidate page, follow “Verify on the
        register”, and read the <code>url</code>, <code>status</code>, and{" "}
        <code>vnd.civicord.person_name</code> text records on the resolver
        contract. Compare them with the claims on the page — they should match
        exactly.
      </p>

      <h2>4. Limits and honesty</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>The scrape is a snapshot: it shows what sites said in April 2025, not today.</li>
        <li>The audit is a snapshot too: a site marked “timed out” may simply have been down that day.</li>
        <li>Records live on the Sepolia testnet — free and public, but a test network, not mainnet.</li>
        <li>Civicord tracks campaign websites only, not social media, leaflets, or hustings claims.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>5. Reproduce it</h2>
      <p>
        Pipeline, audit, frontend, and publishing scripts are open source
        (AGPL-3.0) at <a href="https://github.com/sneldao/civicord">github.com/sneldao/civicord</a>.
        Raw scrape → CSVs → audit → site → on-chain records: every step is
        scripted and re-runnable.
      </p>
    </article>

    <footer>
      <p>
        Civicord record · data: Campaign Lab scrape (April 2025) ·{" "}
        <a href="https://github.com/sneldao/civicord">github.com/sneldao/civicord</a>
      </p>
    </footer>

    <p class="toolbar-hint muted" style="text-align:center">
      Press <kbd>⌘K</kbd> to jump to any candidate, seat, or party — or try it
      against the live data in the <a href="/browse">ledger</a>.
    </p>

    <CommandPalette />
  </body>
</html>
